# Review tiers: how much scrutiny a change needs depends on how much it can break.
#
# GitHub enforces "N required approvals" repo-wide via branch protection,
# not per-path — the two-tier split below only becomes real once a second
# maintainer/counsel reviewer exists to list alongside @mveregge23. Until
# then, every path funnels through the sole maintainer regardless of tier;
# this file documents the intended split so it's a one-line change (add a
# name) rather than a redesign when the contributor base grows.

# Default owner
*                       @mveregge23

# Broker recipes — data, not code. A bad one fails a single broker's jobs, so one
# maintainer approval is enough.
/catalog/brokers/       @mveregge23

# Custom connectors run real code against third-party sites, and legal-basis content
# encodes statutory deadlines and request wording. Two approvals, one of which should
# be counsel for the legal content.
/catalog/connectors/    @mveregge23
/catalog/legal-basis/   @mveregge23

# Database migrations. Same two-approval bar: a bad migration can corrupt the tenant
# boundary itself, and there is no down-migration to undo it with.
/db/migrations/         @mveregge23
